  "transcript_segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 17.92,
      "text": "The Mr. Weaver, I hope this letter finds you well, or at least comfortable enough to"
    },
    {
      "start": 17.92,
      "end": 21.56,
      "text": "hear what the city has to tell."
    },
    {
      "start": 21.56,
      "end": 26.56,
      "text": "The Mr. Weaver, you speak of a fracture, say the whole country's held back by its structure,"
    },
    {
      "start": 26.56,
      "end": 31.16,
      "text": "you pointed the ceiling, the pipes in the floor, and quietly sell off the house to the"
    },
    {
      "start": 31.16,
      "end": 32.16,
      "text": "store."
    },
    {
      "start": 32.16,
      "end": 36.52,
      "text": "You talk about freedom, efficiency speed, but markets grow fat while the six start"
    },
    {
      "start": 36.52,
      "end": 37.52,
      "text": "to bleed."
    },
    {
      "start": 37.52,
      "end": 41.76,
      "text": "The clinics grow thinner, the waiting rooms long, your towers grow higher where developers"
    },
    {
      "start": 41.76,
      "end": 46.92,
      "text": "throw, you split up the tenants, divide up the blame, and somehow the landlord walks"
    },
    {
      "start": 46.92,
      "end": 53.24,
      "text": "out winning the game."
    },
    {
      "start": 53.24,
      "end": 63.76,
      "text": "The Mr. Weaver, who holds your thread, who profits quietly from the words you said."
    },
    {
      "start": 63.76,
      "end": 74.6,
      "text": "The furnace clear, the cloth gets thinner every single year."
    },
    {
      "start": 74.6,
      "end": 79.92,
      "text": "You preach about order, discipline law, about moral decay that you swear that you saw."
    },
    {
      "start": 79.92,
      "end": 85.24,
      "text": "You chase after smoke in a teenager's hand while powder rise by the tongue to the land."
    },
    {
      "start": 85.24,
      "end": 90.56,
      "text": "Your harbor stays busy, your speeches stay neat, the gangs grow louder in every street."
    },
    {
      "start": 90.56,
      "end": 95.08,
      "text": "The sirens keep singing, a midnight refrain, but press rooms insist everything's under"
    },
    {
      "start": 95.08,
      "end": 96.08,
      "text": "control again."
    },
    {
      "start": 96.08,
      "end": 104.4,
      "text": "The rules are strict for the week who survived, but strangely flexible for money that thrives."
    },
    {
      "start": 104.4,
      "end": 110.96,
      "text": "And of course, the sausages, every delay, every bill, every fall, there's always a sausage"
    },
    {
      "start": 110.96,
      "end": 113.92,
      "text": "responsible for it all."
    },
    {
      "start": 113.92,
      "end": 119.84,
      "text": "Remarkable creatures, they seem to control have the parliament hall."
    },
    {
      "start": 119.84,
      "end": 129.84,
      "text": "You miss the Weaver, your loom spins fast, but thread pulled tight, we'll snap at last."
    },
    {
      "start": 129.84,
      "end": 141.64,
      "text": "You miss the Weaver, your cloth may tear, when too many hands find nothing there."
    },
    {
      "start": 141.64,
      "end": 146.6,
      "text": "Strange little shadows stitched into the past, old flags that certain families cast."
    },
    {
      "start": 146.6,
      "end": 151.92,
      "text": "You say that history's buried and gone, yet echoes still hum in the songs you lean on."
    },
    {
      "start": 151.92,
      "end": 157.24,
      "text": "Your party denies every whisper and claim, but sometimes the smoke looks a lot like a flame."
    },
    {
      "start": 157.24,
      "end": 162.52,
      "text": "The slogans feel older than most will admit, just polished and tailored to better fit."
    },
    {
      "start": 162.52,
      "end": 173.36,
      "text": "A suit and a tie can soften a creed, but underneath fabric, the stitches still bleed."
    },
    {
      "start": 173.36,
      "end": 183.36,
      "text": "Sell the rail, sell the care, sell the future piece by piece if it's there."
    },
    {
      "start": 183.36,
      "end": 194.24,
      "text": "Balanced the books, tightened the road, privatized daylight, and then voiced the whole."
    },
    {
      "start": 194.24,
      "end": 205.04,
      "text": "Dear Mr Weaver, answer me true, who keeps the country well-loosen to you."
    },
    {
      "start": 205.04,
      "end": 216.84,
      "text": "Dear Mr Weaver, the pattern shows, the richer the towers, the poorer it grows."
    },
    {
      "start": 216.84,
      "end": 230.84,
      "text": "Dear Mr Weaver, you keep weaving the story, that everything's fine, but the people you're threading are starting to read between the lines."
    }
  ],
